.TH NS 1
.SH NAME
ns \- display name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ns
[
.I pid
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ns
prints the name space of the process named by
.IR pid ,
or of itself with none given, as the
.IR bind (1)
and
.B mount
lines that would rebuild it.
The output comes from reading
.BR /proc/ pid /ns
(see
.IR proc (3))
and is unchanged from what the kernel wrote there.
.PP
Each mount point is one or more lines: the first member plain, a later
member of the same union with
.BR \-a ,
and either with
.B \-c
if it may receive a create.
Replaying the lines in the order printed rebuilds the same union in the
same order.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
ns
bind #/ /
bind #c /dev
bind #b /bin
mount /srv/memfs /mnt
.EE
.SH FILES
.B /proc/*/ns
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/ns/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR ps (1),
.IR proc (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty, or
.B "no such process"
if
.BI /proc/ pid /ns
could not be read.
.SH BUGS
A name longer than ninety-six bytes is kept truncated, with
.B ...
appended.
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR ns ,
this one does not rewrite a network data file's name as its address;
.B /net
carries no such files yet.
